<com:TContent ID="body" >

<h1>Feed Service</h1>
<com:SinceVersion Version="3.1" />
<com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.Services.TFeedService" />

<p class="block-content">
<tt>TFeedService</tt> serves feed content, such as an RSS or an Atom feed. The service
holds a set of feeds. Each feed has an ID and a class that implements
<tt>IFeedContentProvider</tt>. The service parameter in the URL names the feed, so
<tt>index.php?feed=news</tt> serves the feed registered as <tt>news</tt>. No page takes
part in the request.
</p>

<h2>Configuration</h2>
<p class="block-content">
The service is declared in the <a href="?page=Configurations.AppConfig">application
configuration</a>. Each <tt>&lt;feed&gt;</tt> element names a feed by its <tt>id</tt> and
its <tt>class</tt>. Every other attribute is set as a property of the feed object before
the feed runs.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="xml" CssClass="source block-content">
<services>
  <service id="feed" class="Prado\Web\Services\TFeedService">
    <feed id="news" class="Application.Feeds.NewsFeed" Limit="20" />
  </service>
</services>
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">The same configuration in the PHP format:</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
'services' => [
    'feed' => [
        'class' => 'Prado\Web\Services\TFeedService',
        'news' => [
            'class' => 'Application.Feeds.NewsFeed',
            'properties' => ['Limit' => '20'],
        ],
    ],
],
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
In the PHP form each feed is a key of the service entry itself, beside the service's
<tt>class</tt> key, and the initial values of a feed sit under its <tt>properties</tt> key.
The service reads every key of its entry as a feed, so the entry carries the
<tt>class</tt> key and the feeds only. A feed nested under a separate <tt>feed</tt> key is
not found.
</p>

<h2>Writing a feed provider</h2>
<p class="block-content">
A feed class implements <tt>IFeedContentProvider</tt>. It extends
<tt>TApplicationComponent</tt>, or another <tt>TComponent</tt>, so the service can set its
properties. The interface has three methods. <tt>init</tt> receives the feed's own
configuration. <tt>getContentType</tt> returns the content type of the feed.
<tt>getFeedContent</tt> returns the feed as an XML string.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
use Prado\TApplicationComponent;
use Prado\Web\Services\IFeedContentProvider;

class NewsFeed extends TApplicationComponent implements IFeedContentProvider
{
    private $_limit = 10;

    public function getLimit()
    {
        return $this->_limit;
    }

    public function setLimit($value)
    {
        $this->_limit = (int) $value;
    }

    public function init($config)
    {
    }

    public function getContentType()
    {
        return 'application/rss+xml';
    }

    public function getFeedContent()
    {
        $items = '';
        // build one <item> element per entry, at most Limit of them
        return '<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>'
            . '<rss version="2.0"><channel><title>News</title>'
            . $items
            . '</channel></rss>';
    }
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
The content type tells the client which feed format it receives.
</p>
<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Feed format</th><th>Content type</th></tr>
<tr><td>RSS 2.0</td><td><tt>application/rss+xml</tt></td></tr>
<tr><td>RSS 1.0</td><td><tt>application/rdf+xml</tt></td></tr>
<tr><td>Atom</td><td><tt>application/atom+xml</tt></td></tr>
</table>
<p class="block-content">
The service calls <tt>init</tt>, then <tt>getFeedContent</tt>, and writes the content
with the content type from <tt>getContentType</tt>. A request that names a feed the
service does not know receives a 404 error.
</p>

</com:TContent>
